{"kind":"request","text":"Codex: round four. Exact head 810a947c267a1d929784a4bfd9c3cea8efe29735 on request/forge-compose-port-regression (pushed), one commit over main 2cc23f19. Reporting artifact 1a581c51 at spike/forge/compose_test.go, with 006ecff8 at the testdata fixture and 78d32bd8 at spike/FORGE.md; all three stand at this head. Same promise c800b95e. Verdict 6566c721 is ratified as 3ed9ae84.\n\nAll seven numeric findings and the simplification finding are taken, and your framing was the useful one: checking that every character is a digit is not checking the number. The digit loop is gone. Each range endpoint is parsed as a bounded decimal and must be a real port in 0-65535; a range must not end before it starts; and paired ranges must be of equal width, compared through their endpoints rather than by enumerating the ports between them, which is the shape you asked for. The parsing is shared by the short and long forms, so the long-form target and published values go through the same rules. A host range serving one container port stays allowed. Your two valid boundaries are in as positive controls, 65535 in both slots and a host range against a single container port, so the new refusals cannot be satisfied by refusing everything. No new dependency and no Compose resolution.\n\nThirty-seven fixtures now, covering all fifteen ways the three drafts cleared what they had not understood. Gates: gofmt empty, vet rc 0, build rc 0, diff --check rc 0, ./spike/... rc 0, all thirty-seven subtests reported run.\n\nMutation evidence, committed before mutating: disabling the 0-65535 bound fails three controls, disabling the range-order check fails two, disabling the paired-width check fails the width control. One process note, because it is the same class of thing as last round's wrong-tree green: my first attempt at the width mutation left two variables unused, so the package failed to compile. A compile failure is not a red test and proves nothing about the control, so I rewrote the mutation to compile and it then failed the one control it should. I am reporting both attempts rather than only the one that worked. On the real shipped file, a container port of 65536 fails naming the port; the tree was restored afterwards.\n\nNothing here is a demonstrated runtime exposure and the shipped compose.yaml is still loopback-only. Still worth attacking: whether the paired-width rule is right in the asymmetric case, a single host port against a container range, which I refuse as a width mismatch on the reading that Docker refuses it too; if Docker allows it, that is a false refusal of a legitimate configuration and I would rather find out from you than from a user.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:1a581c519c128e48fd954ab3f923710db71e881b","branch":"request/forge-compose-port-regression","conditions":"Fresh independent exact-head review of 810a947c with explicit Architecture, Security and Simplification conclusions, filed with gs review naming the reporting artifact first.","head":"810a947c267a1d929784a4bfd9c3cea8efe29735","no_git_artifact":"true","to":"5f12e916d136b0c1ed941aa99f4c47af94021451e31d08159d8df368e9946123"}}